{"key":"AiDesignerWindow","text":"## AiDesignerWindow\n\nThe **AI Designer** is where you design an AI system as a picture: place\n**Data**, **Model** and **Algorithm** boxes, wire what feeds what, and send the\nwhole design to the xmlIT service with one click. The reply comes back as a\nnode on the same canvas, and any single operation can be tried against the\ngorpIT math service before you commit.\n\n---\n\n@layout\nTitle Bar | 0.50, 0.03 | The gear menu holds New, Open Saved, Open Shared, Library, Import (including Import SAI XML), Focus, Layout, Convert and Window (View Extents, Full Screen, Minimize, halves, quarters, Center — see the UML Window help for the Layout and Window entries). The two code buttons open the XML panel and the well-formedness validator; the map button toggles the minimap. Right-click for Save to GitHub, Export, Undo and Redo.\nRun Bar | 0.50, 0.09 | Run posts the design to xmlIT. Check validates it locally without touching the network. The xmlIT label shows the endpoint; click to edit it, clear it to go back to the Magic Portal. The dropdown on the right switches between the Class, Sequence, Activity and State Machine views.\nConstructs | 0.04, 0.50 | Click Data, Model or Algorithm to drop a new box at the center of the view with starter rows.\nCanvas | 0.45, 0.50 | The design. Drag boxes to move them; the colored lines are derived from the wiring and redraw themselves. Right-click a box to change its construct, add Input or Output ports on an algorithm, or Set Model on a data box. Choose Library in the view dropdown to see every design as a card instead.\nXML Panel | 0.85, 0.50 | The saved document in Tree or Raw form, and a read-only Wire tab showing the exact bytes Run sends.\n\n---\n\n### The three constructs\n\n- **Data** — rows of real values. A data box is *of* a model: right-click it and choose *Set Model* to say which\n- **Model** — the shape of the data: named variables with types and units, and the operations that act on them\n- **Algorithm** — the work. It declares one **Input** model and one **Output** model, its operations, and the flow of expressions that runs them\n\nRight-click any box and use *Change Construct* to turn it into another kind.\n\n### Wiring\n\n- **Input / Output** — right-click an algorithm to choose which model feeds it and which model it fills. These are the only connections you draw; they are saved with the design\n- **Reference** — right-click a variable row and choose *Set Reference* to point it at another model. The reference line is drawn from that row\n- **Instance** — a data box's model draws its own line; you never create it by hand\n- **Compose** — when an algorithm has more than one input, right-click it and choose *Compose inputs into one model*; the designer builds a model of references so the algorithm still has exactly one input\n- Colored lines are derived and redraw on every change. Right-click one to read why it exists\n\n### Running and checking\n\n- **Run** — sends the design and its current values to xmlIT. The result appears as a **Returned** node on the canvas, read-only and never saved with the design\n- **Check** — lists Errors, Warnings and Info in an overlay. Click a finding to jump to its box. The scope line says how many units and collapse policies were checked. Click Check again to close it\n- **Test…** — right-click an operation row to open the Test panel: fill in the values, set an expected result, and *Run test* sends just that expression to gorpIT\n- On the web the endpoint must stay the Magic Portal; a direct address works only in the Unity Editor\n\n### Views\n\n- **Class Diagram** — the boxes and wiring; the only view where you edit\n- **Sequence Diagram** — the calls implied by the Input and Output wiring, in declared order\n- **Activity Diagram** — the values flowing into each algorithm, its operations, and what comes out, each annotated with its unit\n- **State Machine** — a pane under the canvas showing the selected construct's flow as states and guarded transitions. Right-click in the pane to author states and transitions. The step bar walks the flow one transition at a time using the values you type in\n\n### Library\n\nChoose **Library** from the ☰ menu, or open the AI Designer with nothing saved yet, and every design appears as a card on a shelf: **Templates** (the bundled starting points, plus any design a Content Creator or Admin has added), a shelf titled with **your account name** (your designs), **Shared** (the designs everyone sees) and one shelf per **other account** that has designs. Each card shows a picture of the design's canvas, redrawn every time it is saved, so the shelf is a gallery of what you last saw. Tap a card to open it. A design from any shelf but your own opens as a copy in yours, so your saves never change someone else's copy; an Admin also gets *Open in place*. **Drag a card** to move it: onto SHARED, onto your own shelf or onto another account's shelf (an Admin, or whoever put it on the shelf it leaves), after a confirm. Onto TEMPLATES (Content Creators and Admins) it adds a copy and the original stays; from TEMPLATES it brings a copy. The shelf lights when the drop is allowed. **Duplicate** copies beside the original on the same shelf. Right-click a card for **Duplicate**, **Rename…**, **Tags…**, **Share…**, **Add to Templates…** and **Delete…** (or *Remove from Shared…* / *Remove from Templates…*, which whoever put it there may also do; removal asks first and is permanent). A **greyed card** is an incomplete design: it cannot run yet, and the yellow line under its title says where — the same warning the canvas shows. The search box at the top filters every shelf by name, file or construct as you type; the tag chips under it narrow the shelves to designs carrying every lit tag; **Newest / A–Z** switches the order of the account shelves; **Refresh** re-reads the shelves when a design was saved from somewhere else. Click a shelf's header to fold it away; a long shelf shows 24 cards and a \"Show more\" card. The **+ New design** card starts a fresh file.\n\n### Templates\n\nThe **TEMPLATES** shelf is a set of starting points, not documents you share. For a normal account the shelf sits on a lighter ground and says **Copy AI Design Only**: tapping a card copies it into your own designs under *the template's own name* (numbered if you already have one by that name), so what you change is yours and the template stays the same for everyone else.\n\n**Selecting a template opens it LOCKED**, whoever you are. The design appears on the canvas bound to no file and marked as no change at all: the canvas sits on a lighter ground, a padlock badge in its upper left says *Template — view only*, and the construct palette is hidden. You can still **Run** it, **Check** it, **drag the boxes** and use the **Layout** menu — none of that changes the design or is saved — but nothing can be renamed, rewired or written. Open a design of your own, or start a new one, and the lock clears.\n\nFor a **Content Creator** or an **Admin** the shelf is outlined instead of filled and says **Edit Template for All**, and the padlock badge becomes a button: *Unlock to Edit* points the window at the template itself. A bundled template is part of the app and cannot be written, so that binds it to the Templates shelf, where your save replaces the bundled card for every account. A saved template can also be opened in place, renamed, tagged or removed from its card menu. Any design can be put on the shelf with **Add to Templates…**, or by dragging its card onto TEMPLATES.\n\nThe **UNIT TESTS** shelf holds worked examples everyone can see: ten complete designs, simplest first, and two that are supposed to be refused. A **Content Creator** or **Admin** maintains them — selecting one opens the real file for editing, and Rename, Remove and dragging a design onto the shelf all work as they do on Shared. Everyone else views one locked, where **Run** and **Check** still work, and drags a card onto their own shelf for a copy to take apart.\n\n### Describing a design, and what it last did\n\nThe **ⓘ** on the run bar opens the **description** box: each diagram carries its own text and they share one box, so switching view switches the text. Drag its header to move it, its corner to resize, and it stays where you left it. A description is saved with the design, so everyone who opens it reads the same thing — and it is entirely optional.\n\n**⟲ Runs** opens two things. The **expectation** is an editable note saying what this design should produce; it travels with the design. Below it are your **last five runs** of it: when, which build, where it went, how long it took and what came back, with the newest keeping the reply and a Copy button. The run list is yours alone and is never written into the file.\n\n**Every shelf wears a frame that says who may change it.** A FILLED rounded rectangle, with a padlock beside the shelf name and on each card, means nothing there is yours to change — Templates for a normal account, and another account's shelf unless you are an Admin. An OUTLINED rectangle means you can change something there: your own shelf, Shared, and Templates for a Content Creator.\n\n### Schemas and XML documents\n\nAn **XML Schema** (an .xsd file) is the rulebook for a kind of XML document: which parts it has, which are optional, what each value may be. The AI Designer reads one as **models**: every type becomes a Model box, a nested part becomes a line to its own box (with how many there may be), a fixed list of values becomes an enumeration, and a schema that imports other schemas is read as one design, bringing in only the parts it uses.\n\n- **Open Schema Example** (☰ › Open) — six real schemas to explore, from a small client record to a healthcare standard: **Client**, **Tableau Server API**, **EPP Domain** and **EPP Host** (how domain registrars talk to registries), **FHIR Patient** (health records) and the **Holy Bible (KJV)**. Each opens as itself, locked and bound to no file, with a description of what it is and clickable links to where it came from. The padlock's **Edit a Copy** makes it a design of your own\n- Four of them also bring a real document that follows the schema, read in as **Data** — FHIR's sample patient, RFC 5732's name server, three passages of the King James Bible — and FHIR Patient adds an **Algorithm**, CalculateAge, so all three kinds of construct appear together\n- **Import XSD…** (☰ › Import) reads a schema file of your own the same way\n- **Import XML as Data…** (☰ › Import) reads an XML document into the design as Data boxes: its root element names the model, each nested object becomes its own box, and values come from elements, attributes and text. Open the schema it follows first, so the models are there to fill\n\nA link in a description or the run log opens in a new tab when clicked.\n\n### Incomplete designs and the no-fail rule\n\nAn AI design runs only when nothing about it is left unsaid. Every variable must declare a **measurement** (a plain number is `\\one`, not blank) and, once measured, a **collapse** (how its observations merge); every row needs a name and a type; a data value cannot be an empty string; every algorithm needs an Input and an Output model; and the state diagram must check clean. Until then the run bar shows **⚠ Incomplete: <where> — <what>** in yellow, **Run** is greyed (click it, or the warning, to open the full check), and the design's card in the Library is greyed with the same line. Fix what the check names and the warning goes away.\n\n### Files\n\n- Saves as an .xml document in Files/AiDesign under your account; the default name is *AI Design*\n- Closing or minimizing the window, or hiding the browser tab, saves unsaved changes to that file\n- **Import SAI XML** loads a Gestalt or SAI file from the bundled samples and replaces the whole diagram (it asks first when the canvas is not empty)\n- **Open Starter Sample** loads a small worked design to explore\n- **Delete** on the title-bar menu removes the file together with its Library card and picture\n- The XML panel's **Wire** tab is exactly what Run sends: an xaiml envelope around the saved document plus the input values\n\n### Shortcuts\n\nAll shortcuts are shared with the UML Window and pause while you are typing in a field or while the Test or Check panel is open.\n\n- **Ctrl/Cmd+Z** undo, **Ctrl/Cmd+Shift+Z** or **Ctrl/Cmd+Y** redo\n- **Ctrl/Cmd+A / C / X / V / D** — select all, copy, cut, paste, duplicate\n- **Delete / Backspace** — delete the selected boxes\n- **Tab / Shift+Tab** — cycle through boxes; **Esc** clears the selection\n\n### Status: Beta\n### Purpose: AIDesign\n\n---","screenshot":""}